// **A week with a rest day**: the last two days of every seven are free of work, and a person spends them staying put.
//
// This file is an overlay, not an addition. It holds the starter pack's `work` in a form that is shut on the rest days
// (`Input::Week`, `Curve::Days`) and one new activity, `laze`. A host that wants it **replaces** `work` by id and adds
// `laze` (`merge` needs disjoint ids, so it cannot replace), and gates every other workday activity the same way (the
// village pack's `craft` is the other one; the harness's `--weekly` does it):
//
//     let mut file = DefsFile::starter()?;
//     let overlay = DefsFile::from_ron(include_str!("assets/weekly.society.ron"))?;
//     for activity in overlay.activities {
//         match file.activities.iter_mut().find(|a| a.id == activity.id) {
//             Some(slot) => *slot = activity,
//             None => file.activities.push(activity),
//         }
//     }
//     let defs = SocietyDefs::compile(file)?;
//
// Game-day 0 is weekday 0 unless `SocietySettings::start_weekday` says otherwise, and days 5 and 6 are the rest days: a
// host that calls its first day by another name moves the window, not the idea. `docs/research/weekly-rhythm.md` has the
// sources; `docs/loop/runs/2026-10-31-04/` has the measurements, including the two versions of this overlay that failed.
(
    activities: [
        (
            // **Work on workdays only.** The starter's `work` with one more consideration, `Days(0, 5)` on the position in
            // the week: it reads 1 on days 0 to 4 and 0 on days 5 and 6, so a product of considerations is zero on a rest
            // day and the activity is never an option (a window is a hard edge, as `Hours(8, 17)` already is).
            id: "work",
            target: Place("work"),
            duration_hours: 4.0,
            profession: Some("craft"),
            considerations: [
                (input: Hour, curve: Hours(8.0, 17.0)),
                (input: Week, curve: Days(0.0, 5.0)),
            ],
            satisfies: [],
            interaction: None,
            barks: ["Back to it."],
        ),
        (
            // **A rest day is spent staying put.** "During weekends we are more stationary" (Sekara, Stopczynski & Lehmann
            // 2016, supplement, DOI 10.1073/pnas.1602803113). `Itself` stays where the person is, for two hours, in
            // daylight, on a rest day; whoever comes by can still be talked to.
            //
            // **It is here because shutting `work` alone damages the village.** The four hours a workday spends on work go to
            // whatever else is open, and in this pack that is company: with `work` shut and nothing to put in its place, a
            // rest day has 27-30 approaches a villager (a workday has 10) and, over 29 game-days at seed 7, friend ties fall
            // 51 -> 25 and rival ties rise 44 -> 80 (the four villages: friends 51 / 59 / 64 / 58 -> 25 / 21 / 34 / 22).
            // `docs/loop/runs/2026-10-31-04/evidence/attempt1/`. A `laze` that scores by the `fun` need (the obvious
            // version) does not fix it: it fills `fun` and drops out of the draw after a spell, so leisure is 12-14 % of a
            // rest day (3 % of a workday) at any weight from 4 to 16 and a villager still makes 21-23 approaches
            // (`evidence/sweep/fun-*`; those runs also carried a rest-day `visit`, tried for the same paper and dropped:
            // `evidence/sweep/with-visit-*` shows the narrowing without it).
            //
            // So its score is flat (no need term), and **its `weight` is the one number that says how much of a rest day
            // is spent doing nothing**. A design default, chosen by measurement: at seed 7, weights 1 / 3 / 8 give rest-day
            // approaches a villager-day of 11.5 / 7.8 / 6.2 against 10-11 on a workday, different people met a day of
            // 4.4 / 3.4 / 3.1 against 4.2, and rival ties of 35 / 17 / 21 against 44 with no week. 3 is the first at which a
            // rest day is clearly quieter and narrower than a workday (fewer different people, and 49.5 % of approaches to a
            // friend against 40.6 %: Sekara's "a few selected friends"). The price is the `social` need, 0.71 on a rest day
            // against 0.76 on a workday; 8 takes it to 0.64.
            id: "laze",
            weight: 3.0,
            target: Itself,
            duration_hours: 2.0,
            considerations: [
                (input: Week, curve: Days(5.0, 7.0)),
                (input: Hour, curve: Hours(9.0, 21.0)),
            ],
            // A rest: `fun` at 0.4 an hour, 0.8 a two-hour spell against `fun`'s decay of 0.8 a day. Design default.
            satisfies: [("fun", 0.4)],
            interaction: None,
            barks: ["Nowhere to be."],
        ),
    ],
)
